--TEST--
Core\RateLimit::consume throws when the shared store is not there, and never answers allowed — ADR 0075 §§ 3 and 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5 is the load-bearing choice in the whole ADR: a security control that
// fails open silently is worse than none, because the deployment believes it
// has one — and whether *this* limiter should fail open or closed is knowledge
// only the call site has. So the member throws, and the call site decides. This
// case is a call site deciding, which is the shape the ADR argues for rather
// than a message it argues about.
try {
    var $d = Core\RateLimit::consume("account:1", 5, 1m);

    // § 3's four fields, read as members rather than as properties: a
    // `Core`-owned instance has no property a program can reach, which is why
    // this is `->allowed()` and not `->allowed`.
    if ($d->allowed()) {
        echo "allowed, ", $d->remaining(), " of ", $d->limit(), " left", "\n";
    } else {
        echo "refused against a limit of ", $d->limit(), "\n";
    }

    // And `retryAfter` is `?Duration`: absent exactly when the arrival was
    // allowed, rather than a zero the caller has to read as absence.
    var $wait = $d->retryAfter();
    if ($wait == null) {
        echo "no wait", "\n";
    } else {
        echo "wait ", $wait->toSeconds(), "s", "\n";
    }
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    // This application chose to fail closed, visibly. Failing open is the same
    // three lines with the other answer in them, and that is the point.
    echo "quota check unavailable: refused rather than allowed", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
quota check unavailable: refused rather than allowed
